TOPICS=[
('1 · Start in two minutes','''<h2>Create an experiment</h2><ol><li>Start with New assay in the empty workspace or the flask in the left column. Choose the method and protocol.</li><li>Select an assay and enter readings and conditions.</li><li>Save all inputs to a single Excel workbook, including drafts.</li><li>Update and export calculates complete assays and updates one Origin project.</li></ol><p>For a first trial, select the five-method or extended demonstration from the test-tube menu. These are synthetic data.</p>'''),
('2 · Assays and protocols','''<h2>One experiment, several assays</h2><p>Each assay has its own protocol, blanks, calibration and data. Add another assay of the same method for a different protocol or calibration batch. Duplicate creates a separately identified copy; Remove retains history on save.</p><h3>Protocol variants</h3><p>Select an assay and edit its Protocol and conditions tab. Use Save protocol as versioned variant beside the Variant selector, then name the reusable version. The duplicate-assay icon copies a whole assay; it does not save a protocol. New catalogue versions do not change previous experiments. Incomplete TAPPI adaptations require documented operating conditions.</p>'''),
('3 · Enter or paste data','''<h2>One row per reading or weighing</h2><p>Select the reading type from its list. Red asterisks identify required fields. Internal identifiers are automatic. Ctrl+V accepts tab-separated data and decimal point or comma. The row-count selector chooses how many blank rows to add.</p><p>One click selects a cell; typing starts editing. Double-click or F2 edits the existing text. Click a header for field meanings and units. Dimmed fields do not participate in that category. Enter zero explicitly where appropriate; do not replace unknown data with zero.</p><p>Drafts can be saved while you work on other assays. Update does not invent missing measurements.</p>'''),
('4 · Samples and replicates','''<h2>Separate varieties</h2><p>Keep Type = Sample and use different Sample / standard names for different varieties. Within a sample, Biological unit / extraction identifies independent preparations; Reading replicate identifies technical measurements.</p><p>Technical readings are averaged first. Mean, SD and n then refer to independent biological units. Independence cannot be certified by the application. Use the same sample names across FRAP, ABTS and other methods to recognise them in the global summary; their quantities and units remain separate.</p>'''),
('5 · Blanks and calibration','''<h2>FRAP, CUPRAC, TPC and TFC</h2><p>Enter reagent blanks, at least three distinct standard concentrations and samples. Calibration uses unweighted OLS on technical level means with a free intercept.</p><h2>ABTS</h2><p>Radical control without antioxidant contains radical plus solvent at the matched time. Inhibition = 100 × (control − corrected sample)/control. Do not subtract that control again as a reagent blank.</p><p>Matrix correction is additional and net, already corrected for its own blank. Enter 0 when not applicable. Samples share a calibration within an assay; another batch needs another assay.</p>'''),
('6 · Dilution and units','''<h2>Previous dilution</h2><p>1 = undiluted; 2 = one part extract made up to two parts total volume. Multiply successive factors. Do not include dilution on adding assay reagents.</p><p>Before reaction refers to the pipetted vial; Final mixture converts back by total volume/aliquot once. Extract volume is the total extraction volume in mL; reference mass is in g and must correspond to the same preparation and mass basis.</p><p>TPC can convert gallic acid µmol/L to mg/L using 170.12/1000. TFC uses its documented reference; quercetin and rutin equivalents are not interchangeable by relabelling.</p>'''),
('7 · DPPH kinetics and EC50','''<h2>Not a Trolox calibration</h2><ol><li>Enter several doses in mg dry extract/mL of FINAL mixture.</li><li>Record real times and absorbances for each dose and replicate.</li><li>Include radical controls at matching times.</li><li>Confirm the plateau once at the final reading of each series after checking stability.</li></ol><p>Technical plateau means are interpolated locally around 50% inhibition for each biological unit. Monotonicity and a bracket around 50% are required. There is no extrapolation or automatic plateau detection. A missing estimate remains explicit.</p>'''),
('8 · Weighing and sieving','''<h2>Moisture, dry matter and ash</h2><p>Enter container tare m0, container + initial sample m1 and container + final residue m2 in grams. Require m0≤m2≤m1 and m1>m0. Dry-basis ash requires the corresponding dry fraction, 0–1; avoid double correction.</p><h2>Particle size distribution</h2><p>One row per fraction and sieving unit. Enter actual lower/upper apertures in µm, retained mass and total loaded mass. Include the collector pan. Use another biological unit for an independent sieving run. Recovery and losses remain visible; percentages are not renormalised to 100%.</p>'''),
('9 · Save, Excel and Origin','''<h2>Files and updates</h2><p>Save stores inputs and marks calculation pending. Update and export uses the saved workbook; save your window changes and close Excel/Origin documents first.</p><p>Excel has Index, global summary and prefixed tabs per assay. Edit RAW and Parameters values, preserving sheet names and headers. Origin uses PhDexLab / assay / Data and Graphs. Figures and CSVs are separated by assay.</p><p>The figures button opens Figures when several assays are present, or that assay’s figure folder when there is only one. Reopen after external edits to avoid overwriting newer input. Refresh is explicit. Verified unchanged results can be reused; the update button’s context menu can force a full rebuild.</p>'''),
('10 · Review issues','''<h2>States</h2><p>Draft: inputs or method conditions missing. Pending: saved, awaiting calculation. Calculated: review QC. Review: at least one estimate needs attention. Error: inspect the activity log and preserve files.</p><p>High R² alone is insufficient: review slope, range, replicates, recovery, matrix absorbance and units. No automatic point exclusion or significance tests are performed. The progress bar represents phases, not a predicted completion time. Software verification does not validate your analytical method.</p>'''),
('11 · Data templates','''<h2>Reuse structure safely</h2><p>The grid icon opens Save / Load data template. By default, sample names, replicate structure, doses, times and standard concentrations are retained while measurements and plateau confirmations are cleared. Retaining measurements is an explicit option and remains labelled.</p><p>Templates are compatible only with the same method and table structure. Loading can replace or append rows, always assigning new internal IDs. Protocol conditions remain those of the selected assay. Review duplicates when appending.</p>'''),
('12 · Appearance and figure styles','''<h2>Two independent choices</h2><p>The header language selector switches the interface between Spanish and English. The sun/moon button switches light/dark mode. These preferences are retained locally and do not change scientific figure labels or experimental data.</p><p>The palette icon offers ten base presets. Results, Calibration, Dose curves and Typography tabs independently control palettes, marker shape, open/solid symbols, sizes, lines and fonts. One colour per sample distinguishes varieties. Edit individual colours and save, import or export JSON styles without experimental data. The selected preset is saved with the experiment and applied to managed Origin and Publication graphs on the next update. Choosing a different preset replaces managed graph formatting; otherwise existing editable Origin formatting is preserved. Figure labels remain in scientific English.</p><h2>Application updates</h2><p>The header update button shows the installed version and checks the latest stable release from RpastranaE/PhDexLab. The portable executable can download automatically in the background, verify SHA-256 and install on exit. Only Application is replaced. Startup is checked and a failed installation restores the previous version. You can disable automatic updates. Experimental data are never transmitted. Network errors do not prevent local work.</p>'''),
]
